---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";

const title = "What Is Swing Trading?";
const description =
  "Swing trading explained: holding period, how it differs from intraday and " +
  "investing, what the approach actually requires, and its honest costs.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description} crumb="Trading / Swing trading">
  <h1>What is swing trading?</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Swing trading means holding a position for several days to a few weeks,
    aiming to capture one directional move rather than a long-term thesis or an
    intraday fluctuation. It sits between day trading and investing, and it
    inherits problems from both.
  </p>

  <h2>Where it sits</h2>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>intraday</code><span class="meta">minutes to hours</span></div>
      <p>Positions closed before the session ends. Costs dominate, since you pay
        the spread and brokerage repeatedly against small moves.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>swing</code><span class="meta">days to weeks</span></div>
      <p>Overnight risk is accepted in exchange for larger moves and far fewer
        transactions. Daily bars are usually enough.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>positional_and_investing</code><span class="meta">months to years</span></div>
      <p>Held on a view about the business rather than the price pattern.
        Different questions entirely — see
        <a href="/learn/evaluating-a-business/">evaluating a business</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>What the approach assumes</h2>
  <p>
    Swing trading rests on one premise: that price moves are not entirely random
    over a horizon of days to weeks, and that some of the structure is
    identifiable in advance. That premise is defensible but weak — the observable
    edge is small, and it is not present in all conditions.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    It is emphatically not the premise that indicators predict prices. What
    trend-following approaches actually rely on is that a move already underway is
    somewhat more likely to continue than to reverse immediately, which is a much
    more modest claim.
  </p>

  <h2>The honest arithmetic</h2>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>win_rates_are_low</code><span class="meta">by design</span></div>
      <p>Trend-following approaches typically win 35–45% of the time, and make
        money through a payoff ratio above 2:1. That is not a flaw; it is how the
        structure works. It does mean long losing streaks are routine.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>costs_matter_more_than_expected</code><span class="meta">the usual killer</span></div>
      <p>Brokerage, STT, stamp duty and slippage on every round trip. On illiquid
        stocks slippage alone can exceed the edge. A strategy that works at 0.2%
        round-trip costs can lose to an index fund at 1%.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>regime_dependence</code><span class="meta">the structural weakness</span></div>
      <p>Trend-following needs trends. In a sideways or narrow market the same
        rules produce a stream of small losses, and no amount of parameter tuning
        fixes it — the conditions simply are not there.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>taxation</code><span class="meta">India</span></div>
      <p>Positions held under a year attract short-term capital gains treatment,
        which is less favourable than long-term. Frequent trading means the tax
        drag is continuous. Verify current rates before planning around them.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>What it actually requires</h2>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>written_rules</code><span class="meta">non-negotiable</span></div>
      <p>Entry, exit, position size and stop, decided before the position exists.
        Rules made during a trade get renegotiated.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>a_record</code><span class="meta">the thing almost nobody keeps</span></div>
      <p>Every trade logged with its reason, size, and outcome. Without one you
        have impressions rather than evidence, and impressions are reliably
        flattering.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>capital_you_can_leave_alone</code><span class="meta">practical</span></div>
      <p>A 20–30% drawdown is a normal event, not a disaster. Money needed within
        the year does not belong here.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>An honest assessment</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    A well-constructed swing strategy on Indian equities might produce a Sharpe
    ratio near 1 on tradeable names after realistic costs. That is a genuine but
    modest edge — comparable to the index on return with meaningfully smaller
    drawdowns, or slightly better, and considerably more work.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    Anyone promising much more than that is either measuring badly or selling
    something. <a href="/learn/backtesting/">Backtesting</a> covers the specific
    errors that make results look far better than they are.
  </p>

  <div class="ad">Advertisement</div>

  <p class="lede">
    Next: <a href="/learn/risk-management/">risk management</a> ·
    <a href="/learn/backtesting/">backtesting</a> ·
    <a href="/learn/trading-psychology/">trading psychology</a>
  </p>
</Base>
